# Developer notes — API assumptions and pre-flight checks

Fusion cannot run in CI or in a cloud session, so the parts of this add-in
that depend on Fusion API behavior are listed here with the snippet that
verifies them. Re-run them in **Utilities → Text Commands** (switch the
input mode from `Txt` to `Py`) when a new Fusion release misbehaves.

## 1. Canonical frame

Everything is built in a new component created under the active component:
the part origin at the component origin, length along +X, width along +Y,
the top face on the XY plane and the body hanging below it (−Z). Sketches
are native to that component, so `modelToSketchSpace` works in component
space and no occurrence/proxy mapping is needed.

## 2. Extrude direction is probed, not assumed

`_extrude_body` extrudes the outline by −thickness first and checks the
body's bounding box (`maxPoint.z <= 1e-4`, i.e. the body hangs below the
sketch plane); a body that comes out above the plane is deleted and redone
with the opposite sign. Cuts into the body try −depth first, then +depth.

## 3. `isValidResult` keeps the preview and skips execute

`on_preview` sets `args.isValidResult = True` after a successful build, so
Fusion reuses the preview as the final result and does not fire execute.
`on_execute` keeps a `_preview_ok` guard as a fallback either way.

## 4. Metadata and edit mode

Layout parameters and the entity tokens of every created object are stored
as JSON in an attribute on the outline sketch (`constants.ATTRIBUTE_GROUP`
/ `ATTRIBUTE_NAME`, version `constants.METADATA_VERSION`). Rebuilding keeps
that sketch object alive (its curves are replaced in place) and deletes the
other tracked objects feature-first (`_delete_tracked`), then recreates them
in the same component.

## 5. Settings

`settings.json` next to the add-in holds the last-used dialog values, as the
expressions the user typed. The `*_INPUTS` tables at the top of the command
module are the single settings-key ↔ input-id mapping; a new persisted input
is added to a table *and* to `on_create`. Only `on_destroy` writes the file,
and only when the dialog completed (OK) and was fully built.

## Pre-flight: a sketch on an offset plane maps a known point to z ≈ 0

```python
import adsk.core, adsk.fusion
app = adsk.core.Application.get()
d = adsk.fusion.Design.cast(app.activeProduct)
root = d.rootComponent
pi = root.constructionPlanes.createInput()
pi.setByOffset(root.xYConstructionPlane, adsk.core.ValueInput.createByReal(-0.6))
plane = root.constructionPlanes.add(pi)
sk = root.sketches.add(plane)
print(sk.modelToSketchSpace(adsk.core.Point3D.create(0, 0, -0.6)).z)  # ~0 if the sign is as assumed
```
